<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=perfume">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Chemistry</p><h1 class="enc-title">Perfume</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 2000 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/21st-century-bce">21st century BCE</a> · Also known as perfumery</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Perfume is a mixture of fragrant essential oils or aroma compounds, fixatives and solvents, used to give an agreeable scent to the body, objects and living spaces. Perfumery began in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus-valley-civilisation">Indus Valley civilisation</a> and possibly ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. In 2003 archaeologists uncovered what are believed to be the oldest surviving perfumes, more than 4,000 years old, in a perfumery at Pyrgos, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a>, with at least 60 stills. A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuneiform">cuneiform</a> tablet of the 2nd millennium BCE mentions the perfume maker Tapputi. Modern perfumery began in the late nineteenth century with the synthesis of aroma compounds such as vanillin and coumarin.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/perfume.jpg" alt="Perfume" width="330" height="330" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Takkk · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Perfume_set_from_Sovjetunio_cca_1965.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>c. 2000 BCE</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>Pyrgos, Cyprus</dd></div><div><dt>Credited to</dt><dd>Tapputi</dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Also arose independently</span>Mesopotamia (2nd millennium BCE); Indus Valley (3300-1300 BCE)</p><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>Perfumery began in Mesopotamia, Egypt, the Indus Valley and possibly ancient China, with no date given for the start. The Indus civilisation is given as 3300 BCE to 1300 BCE, which is not a date for perfume itself.</p><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=perfume">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
